      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a4.24.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g4.24.1', 'g4.24.8', 'g4.24.10'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "In spirit and truth",
          body: "The woman asks *where* — which mountain, whose temple. Jesus answers *how*, and dissolves the question. **“God is spirit”** — not confined to a place, not reachable by geography — so worship cannot finally be about a site at all. To worship **in spirit** is to worship out of the new birth of chapter 3, animated by God’s own Spirit; **in truth** is to worship the God truly disclosed, now in the Son (“I am the truth,” 14:6). Note the hinge in between: *“salvation is from the Jews”* (v. 22) — Jesus does not flatten the two temples into “all roads lead to God”; the true worship comes through Israel’s Messiah, and then bursts every boundary. The stunning thing is *to whom* he says it: a Samaritan woman of doubtful reputation, at a foreign well, is told the deepest thing about worship in the Gospel — and that **the Father is seeking** worshipers like her. Access to God is being unhooked from holy places and reattached to a person.",
        },
      ],
